[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  hello everyone my name is nari sol i'm a  pianist composer and what you just heard  was a little tune i wrote this week  after spending some time listening to  retro video game music i first went into  this with a long list of suggestions  from you all including all kinds of  video game music but for now i narred it  down to 8-bit music  also known as chiptune  [Music]  some general characteristics that come  to mind are that a lot of these tunes  are very simple they're very catchy  they're very easy to recognize  [Music]  and there's little to no development and  i think that's because functionally  speaking these are tunes that accompany  a game whether it's while you're playing  it or before so i think that helps keep  that tune neutral to when you're hearing  it within that context of the game after  doing a lot of listening i went onto  youtube of course to look for tutorials  on how to simulate these sounds on logic  and i played around with that for quite  a bit  so let's start out with uh logic  quick on zero this one  takes a long time to fade out  so right now i'm just playing with the  different dials  and i'm experimenting with the different  sounds  [Music]  something i find very charming about a  lot of the tracks that i've been  listening to  is that there are a lot of effects  within the track that  maybe are effects within the the game  itself  maybe something like this  is a sort of laser beam i also hear a  lot of runs  something like that  there are a lot of other quirky sounds  that i can think of that  maybe are just characteristic of this  type of style  [Music]  there aren't too many instances where i  hear very thick full cords and i think  that's because of  the natural limitations of the type of  technology the decay or non-decay if you  said it that way  is not that pleasant  a lot of that also depends on the type  of melody for percussion i'm usually  hearing this noise  which is a sound created by pushing up  the noise toggle all the way up  to create this  static sound i also hear a lot of  repeated notes  whether it's going back and forth  between  two notes or  an octave  [Music]  those things being said i found this  genre to be very open to many different  possibilities not just within the  different sounds being used but also  with the different types of melodies  harmonies and styles within this genre  so here's just a very quick overview of  the different tracks that go into this  little tune of mine first of all the  percussion  i'm just using a very quick and dry  static sound  the bass line uses this type of sound  [Music]  a little more resonant  [Music]  on the off beats i'm going back and  forth between a minor third and a major  third  [Laughter]  also accompanying that i have this line  and on the top i'm using this type of  sound  [Music]  i'm also using this for effects such as  this run  and chords like this  and that's about it anyway thank you so  much for watching i really appreciate  all of your comments i read through them  i try my best  and also if you're interested in my book  or sheet music from these videos check  out my website  [Music]  you

